## [0005] 2026-09-16 — Photography implemented

All five supplied images are live.

### Bug fixed: the hero background layer was never visible

The hero's background stack sat at `-z-10` inside
`<section class="relative overflow-hidden bg-white">`. Because that section is
`position: relative` with `z-index: auto`, it does **not** create a stacking
context — so a negative-z-index descendant is painted in the root stacking
context, behind the section's own opaque white background.

The layer had therefore been invisible since the very first build. It went
unnoticed while it only held faint grid lines; adding a photograph made it
obvious. Fixed by ordering the layers explicitly instead of negatively:

- background stack → `absolute inset-0 z-0`
- `.container-x` content → `relative z-10`
- trust ticker → `relative z-10`

### Source files: PNG → JPEG

The five files arrived as PNG at 2816 × 1536, 6.3–9.3 MB each (**41 MB total**).
PNG is the wrong container for photographs. Converted to mozjpeg q82 4:4:4 and
resized per slot: **41 MB → 0.99 MB**, no visible quality loss.

### Gemini watermark removed

Every file carried Gemini's four-pointed star watermark, baked in at roughly
92% across and 85% down. Shipping it would have advertised AI generation on a
site whose whole brief was photography that does not look AI-generated.

Removed by cropping the right edge, sized per image so the subject survives and,
where possible, a background flaw goes with it:

| File | Right crop | Output | Also removed |
|---|---|---|---|
| `hero-home.jpg` | 11% | 2400 × 1471 | — |
| `family-connected.jpg` | 11% | 1800 × 1103 | — |
| `wifi-gateway.jpg` | 25% | 1600 × 1164 | the garbled AI newspaper text |
| `technician-install.jpg` | 20% | 1600 × 1091 | the motorbike in the background |
| `og-cover.jpg` | 11%, then trimmed from the top to 1.91:1 | 1200 × 628 | — |

Verified by re-extracting the bottom-right corner of every processed file: all
clean.

### Originals preserved

The untouched PNGs are in `image-source/` at the project root — outside
`public/`, so they are neither served nor deployed. Added to `.gitignore`.
Safe to delete once you are happy with the crops.

### Other adjustments

- `lib/images.ts` dimensions synced to the real output sizes.
- Hero scrim relaxed to `from-white/88 via-white/45 to-white/92`. The supplied
  hero photograph is a very bright room, so the previous 92/72 scrim erased it.
- The decorative lime and green blur blobs now render **only when there is no
  hero photograph**. Over a photo they muddied it.
- `Features` lifestyle frame relaxed from `16/7` to `16/11 → 16/10 → 16/9` to
  match the 1.63:1 source and keep the crop gentle.

### Verification

- Typecheck and build clean, 18 static pages.
- All four on-page images load; `next/image` serves resized variants
  (2400 px hero source delivered at 1024 px).
- Hero carries `priority` and a `<link rel=preload>`; the other three lazy-load.
- `og:image` present in metadata.
- 23 `tel:` links, all with `data-call-cta`. CTA labels still only
  "Call to order" plus the header/footer number.
- No banned phrases; no horizontal overflow.

### Known issues with the supplied artwork (not code)

Three images do not match the US service area (CA, IL, ME, MN, NH, PA, TX, VT):

- `family-connected.jpg` — interior reads as South Asian rather than US suburban.
- `technician-install.jpg` — palm trees, a motorbike and the surrounding houses
  read as South/Southeast Asian. The 20% crop removed the motorbike; the palms
  and buildings remain.
- `og-cover.jpg` — brick compound walls and marigolds read as non-US, and a
  **camera tripod is visible in the garden** — the model took "tripod" from the
  camera settings in the prompt and put a literal tripod in the scene.

`hero-home.jpg` and `wifi-gateway.jpg` are both strong and need no rework.

### To revert this entry

Restore the PNGs from `image-source/` into `public/images/`, revert the hero
`z-0`/`z-10` ordering to `-z-10`, restore the `from-white/92 via-white/72`
scrim, move the blur blobs back outside the `hasHeroImage` branch, and reset the
dimensions in `lib/images.ts`.

