## [0004] 2026-09-16 — Photography slots wired (files not yet supplied)

Five image slots added. **Drop the files into `public/images/` using the exact
filenames below — nothing else needs editing.**

| Slot | Filename | Size | Where it appears |
|---|---|---|---|
| `hero` | `hero-home.jpg` | 2400 × 1350 | Full-bleed behind the hero, under a white scrim |
| `lifestyle` | `family-connected.jpg` | 1800 × 1000 | Wide band in the "Why fiber" section |
| `gateway` | `wifi-gateway.jpg` | 1600 × 1200 | Equipment band above the specifications table |
| `install` | `technician-install.jpg` | 1600 × 1200 | Beside the closing block in "How it works" |
| `og` | `og-cover.jpg` | 1200 × 630 | Social share card (metadata only, never on-page) |

### New files

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `lib/images.ts` | The manifest: filename, alt text and dimensions per slot. Client-safe (no `fs`). |
| `lib/images.server.ts` | `hasImage()` / `imageFlags()` — build-time filesystem checks. Marked `server-only` so it can never be pulled into a client bundle. |

### Graceful absence

Every slot is guarded by `hasImage()`, evaluated **at build time**. A slot whose
file is missing falls back to the previous treatment:

- hero → the original grid-line background
- lifestyle / gateway / install → the band is omitted entirely
- og → the `og:image` meta tag is simply not emitted

So the site never shows a broken image, an empty frame or a alt-text stub while
the artwork is still being produced. Verified: with `public/images/` empty the
build emits **0 `<img>` tags** and every section still renders.

### Implementation notes

- `next/image` throughout, so files are converted to AVIF/WebP and resized per
  breakpoint automatically. A 2400 px hero source was served at 1024 px in
  testing.
- The hero uses `priority` (it is the LCP element) and gets a `<link rel=preload>`.
  The other three are lazy-loaded — confirmed they do not fetch until scrolled to.
- `sharp` installed, which `next/image` requires for production optimisation.
- `server-only` installed to guard `lib/images.server.ts`.
- Hero is a client component, so `app/page.tsx` resolves `hasImage('hero')` on
  the server and passes it down as the `hasHeroImage` prop.

### Hero scrim

A single gradient — `from-white/92 via-white/72 to-white`. One layer, so the
coverage is predictable: heavy at the top to keep the sticky header clean,
lightest through the middle where the photograph should read, solid white at the
bottom to blend into the next section.

**To show more of the photograph, lower the `via-white/72` stop** in
`components/sections/Hero.tsx`. To gain headline contrast, raise it.

### To revert this entry

Delete `lib/images.ts`, `lib/images.server.ts` and `public/images/`, remove the
`Image` imports and `hasImage(...)` blocks from `Hero.tsx`, `Features.tsx`,
`FinePrint.tsx`, `HowItWorks.tsx` and `layout.tsx`, and drop the `hasHeroImage`
prop from `app/page.tsx`. `npm uninstall sharp server-only`.

